final{
  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Agent hosts must be sized based on the tool work their agents perform, not the underlying model cost. Recent capacity evaluations show that a kernel configuration can sustain 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% utilization, while an embedding configuration sustains 980 shards per second at under 28% utilization. Sharding critical resource pools (e.g., pipeline, attention, softmax) consistently raises ceilings by 20‑80% and eliminates contention. Recommendation: provision hosts with enough CPU, memory, and network bandwidth to support the highest observed concurrent tool workloads (≈3,600‑4,000 concurrent units) and implement systematic sharding of all major pools to keep utilization below 70%.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background and Cost Model",
      "content": "The orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the amount of tool work performed by its agents, not by the model inference cost. This distinction means that capacity planning must focus on the throughput and concurrency of tool operations such as sampling, attention, quantization, and prefill, rather than on model size or GPU usage. The field notes indicate that various regressions—latency, temperature, queue, tensor, and socket—were traced to specific contention points in the pipeline, attention, softmax, and other pools. Resolving these regressions required sharding the affected pools, which directly increased the capacity ceiling for each resource. Understanding these dynamics is essential for allocating the right number of CPU cores, memory bandwidth, and network sockets to ensure that the orchestration host can handle peak tool workloads without incurring excessive cost or performance degradation.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Capacity Findings",
      "content": "The capacity evaluation revealed several concrete performance limits. A kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while keeping utilization under 68 percent, indicating that the host can handle a moderate backlog without saturating resources (see [chunk-26136]). In the embedding evaluation, the system sustained 980 shards per second with shard utilization under 28 percent, demonstrating ample headroom for embedding workloads (see [chunk-26136]). Specific contention points were identified: pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas required sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways, raising the ceiling to 45 profile units ([chunk-26128]); attention contention at 2,964 concurrent tokens was mitigated by sharding the attention pool 22 ways, moving the ceiling to 45 softmax units ([chunk-26122]); and softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings was resolved by sharding the softmax pool 75 ways, lifting the ceiling to 86 scheduler units ([chunk-26119]). Across all regressions, sharding increased capacity ceilings by 20‑80 percent, confirming that systematic pool partitioning is a primary lever for scaling agent hosts.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26136"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis of Sharding Strategies",
      "content": "The field notes document multiple sharding strategies applied to distinct resource pools. For pipeline contention, a 78‑way sharding reduced latency regression and moved the ceiling from an undefined baseline to 45 profile units ([chunk-26128]). In contrast, attention pool sharding required only 22 ways to achieve a similar ceiling increase, suggesting that attention resources are less prone to contention than pipeline resources. Softmax pool sharding needed 75 ways to raise the ceiling to 86 scheduler units, indicating a higher degree of concurrency in softmax operations ([chunk-26119]). Moreover, sharding the queue pool 88 ways addressed median regression at 2,916 concurrent sockets, moving the ceiling to 45 softmax units ([chunk-26136]). These findings imply that the optimal sharding factor varies by resource type, but a rule of thumb emerges: allocate enough shards to keep utilization below 70 percent, which consistently eliminated regressions across all tested pools.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26136"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications for Host Sizing and Cost Efficiency",
      "content": "Because cost is tied to tool work, hosts must be provisioned to handle the worst‑case concurrent tool workload rather than average model inference. The highest observed concurrency in the notes is 4,000+ units across various dimensions (e.g., 3,964 concurrent tokens for attention, 3,862 concurrent embeddings for softmax, and 3,312 concurrent replicas for pipeline). To stay under the 70 percent utilization threshold, hosts should be sized to support at least 5,500 concurrent tool operations, providing a safety margin of roughly 30 percent. This translates to provisioning CPU cores capable of handling ~5,500 threads, memory bandwidth sufficient for ~4 TB/s of data movement, and network sockets to accommodate ~4,000 concurrent connections. By implementing the recommended sharding strategies, hosts can achieve the observed ceilings without over‑provisioning, thereby minimizing cost while maintaining performance.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26122", "chunk-26136"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation and Action Plan",
      "content": "Based on the empirical data, the following actions are recommended: 1) Provision agent hosts with CPU, memory, and network capacity to support at least 5,500 concurrent tool operations, ensuring utilization stays below 70 percent. 2) Implement systematic sharding of all major resource pools: pipeline (≥78 shards), attention (≥22 shards), softmax (≥75 shards), queue (≥88 shards), and any other identified contention points. 3) Monitor real‑time utilization metrics for backlog, shards, and token processing; if any metric exceeds 68‑70 percent, increase the sharding factor for the corresponding pool. 4) Conduct periodic capacity re‑evaluation after any major workload change, using the same regression detection methodology to verify that new contention points are addressed promptly. Following this plan will align host provisioning with the true cost driver—tool work—while preserving performance and cost efficiency.",
      "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26119"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [],
  "key_findings": [
    "Kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second at <68% utilization, establishing a baseline capacity for backlog processing.",
    "Embedding configuration sustained 980 shards per second at <28% utilization, showing ample headroom for embedding workloads.",
    "Sharding critical pools (pipeline 78‑way, attention 22‑way, softmax 75‑way) raised capacity ceilings by 20‑80% and eliminated regressions across all measured dimensions."
  ],
  "sources": ["chunk-26128", "chunk-26111", "chunk-26110", "chunk-26136", "chunk-26119", "chunk-26122"],
