  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Agent hosts must be sized based on the tool work their agents perform, not the underlying model cost. Recent capacity evaluations show that a kernel configuration can sustain 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% utilization, while an embedding configuration sustains 980 shards per second at under 28% utilization. Sharding critical resource pools (e.g., pipeline, attention, softmax) resolves contention and lifts ceilings dramatically, enabling up to 45× higher concurrency in some dimensions. The recommendation is to provision hosts with enough CPU, memory, and network to support the highest observed contention points—specifically pipeline, attention, and softmax pools—while applying the proven sharding ratios to maintain utilization below 70%.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background and Cost Model",
      "content": "The orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the tool work performed by its agents, not by the model itself. This distinction means that capacity planning must focus on the computational and I/O demands of the tools rather than the inference cost of the language model. In practice, the agents execute a variety of tasks—such as prefill, sampling, quantization, and embedding—that each impose distinct resource pressures. For example, the pipeline pool experienced contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas, which was mitigated by sharding the pool 78 ways, dramatically increasing throughput. Understanding these tool-specific workloads is essential for accurate budgeting and for avoiding hidden cost escalations that arise when agents exceed their designed capacity limits.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Capacity Findings",
      "content": "Extensive measurements across multiple resource dimensions reveal the specific limits and the effectiveness of sharding strategies. The kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while keeping utilization under 68%, indicating a comfortable headroom for typical workloads. In the embedding evaluation, the system handled 980 shards per second with utilization below 28%, suggesting that embedding workloads are less CPU-bound but may be memory-intensive. Critical regressions were identified and resolved: pipeline contention at 3,312 replicas was alleviated by 78-way sharding; attention contention at 2,544 concurrent sampling required a 10-way attention pool split; and softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings was mitigated by a 75-way softmax pool split. These interventions moved performance ceilings dramatically—e.g., the latency ceiling rose to 45 profile units after sharding the pipeline pool, and the softmax ceiling increased to 86 scheduler units after sharding. The data also show that specific thresholds trigger degradation: cache memory degrades beyond 4,133 backlog units, and attention latency worsens past 2,182 saturation units, prompting recommended budget increases for those resources.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26119]", "[chunk-26122]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis of Resource Pools",
      "content": "When comparing the impact of sharding across resource pools, the most pronounced gains appear in pools that were originally bottlenecked by high concurrency. The pipeline pool, after being split into 78 shards, lifted the latency ceiling from a baseline of 30 to 45 profile units, representing a 50% increase in capacity. The attention pool, sharded 10 ways, eliminated a variance regression that had manifested at 254 concurrent samples, allowing the system to sustain higher sampling rates without variance spikes. Softmax, which originally capped at 30 saturation units, benefited from a 75-way split, moving the ceiling to 86 scheduler units—a nearly threefold improvement. Conversely, pools with lower contention, such as the quantization pool (sharded 18 ways) and the replica pool (sharded 18 ways), showed modest gains, indicating that sharding should be prioritized for the most contended resources to maximize cost efficiency.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26119]", "[chunk-26100]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications for Host Sizing and Utilization",
      "content": "The findings imply that agent hosts should be provisioned with sufficient headroom to accommodate peak contention scenarios. Specifically, hosts must support at least 3,862 concurrent embedding operations (softmax pool) and 3,312 concurrent replicas (pipeline pool) before sharding, which translates to a baseline CPU core count of roughly 64 cores when accounting for 2 threads per core and a 70% utilization target. Memory requirements should reflect the cache and attention budgets: raise dataset memory to 62 units and attention latency budget to 39 units to avoid degradation beyond 4,133 backlog and 2,182 saturation thresholds, respectively. Network bandwidth must also be provisioned for up to 980 shard operations per second, ensuring that latency remains under 68% utilization. By applying the recommended sharding ratios—pipeline 78-way, attention 10-way, softmax 75-way—hosts can maintain utilization below 70% even under peak loads, preserving performance and controlling cost.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26108]", "[chunk-26132]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation and Action Plan",
      "content": "Based on the data, the clear recommendation is to provision agent hosts with the following baseline specifications: • CPU: 64 physical cores (128 logical threads) to handle peak concurrency in pipeline and softmax pools. • Memory: 256 GB, with an additional 62 GB allocated for dataset cache and 39 GB for attention latency buffers. • Network: 10 Gbps NIC to sustain up to 1,150 backlog and 980 shard operations per second. • Storage: NVMe SSDs with at least 2 TB capacity to support high‑throughput prefill and embedding workloads. Implement sharding configurations as follows: – Pipeline pool: 78-way sharding – Attention pool: 10-way sharding – Softmax pool: 75-way sharding – Quantization pool: 18-way sharding – Replica pool: 18-way sharding These settings keep utilization under 70% across all measured dimensions, prevent regressions, and align host cost directly with tool work rather than model inference. The action plan includes: 1. Deploy the specified hardware baseline. 2. Configure the sharding ratios in the orchestration layer. 3. Monitor the key metrics (backlog per second, shard per second, utilization percentages) for a 30‑day trial. 4. Adjust memory budgets if cache or attention latency thresholds are approached. 5. Re‑evaluate cost per workflow after the trial to confirm that tool‑driven costs remain within budget.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26120]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    }
  ],
  "code_snippets": [],
  "key_findings": [
    "Kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second at <68% utilization, demonstrating headroom for typical workloads.",
    "Sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways lifted the latency ceiling from 30 to 45 profile units, a 50% capacity increase.",
    "Softmax pool sharding 75 ways moved the saturation ceiling from 30 to 86 scheduler units, nearly tripling throughput capacity."
  ],
  "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26119]", "[chunk-26122]", "[chunk-26100]"],
