      "title": "Implications for Host Sizing and Utilization",
      "content": "The data underscores three key implications for capacity planning. First, host sizing should be based on the peak tool‑work throughput rather than model inference cost. The kernel configuration’s ability to sustain 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% utilization provides a concrete benchmark for the maximum sustainable load. Second, sharding is an essential technique; without it, many pools would hit contention ceilings far below the observed capacities. For instance, without sharding, pipeline contention would limit concurrency to a fraction of the 45‑profile ceiling achieved after 78‑way sharding ([chunk-26128]). Third, maintaining utilization below 70% offers a safety margin for unexpected spikes, as demonstrated by the embedding configuration’s comfortable 28% utilization even at near‑full shard throughput.\n\nThese implications suggest that a host provisioned to handle roughly 1,200 backlog operations per second (providing a 5% buffer over the observed 1,149) and equipped with sharding capabilities for the identified pools will meet current and near‑future demands. Additionally, monitoring tools should track the specific contention metrics (e.g., concurrent replicas, tokens, embeddings) to trigger dynamic sharding adjustments as workloads evolve.",
