  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Agent hosts must be sized based on the tool work their agents perform, not the underlying model cost. Recent capacity evaluations show that a kernel configuration can sustain 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% utilization, while an embedding configuration sustains 980 shards per second at under 28% utilization. Sharding contention points—pipeline, attention, quantization, and others—must be addressed to maintain these performance levels. Recommendation: provision hosts to handle at least 1,200 concurrent tool operations with sharding across the identified pools, and allocate additional memory budgets as suggested by the dataset and attention teams.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background and Cost Model",
      "content": "The orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work performed by its agents, not by the model itself. This distinction shifts capacity planning focus from model inference costs to the throughput and contention characteristics of the tool pipelines. The field notes reveal multiple regression points where specific pools (e.g., pipeline, attention, quantization) become bottlenecks under high concurrency. Understanding these bottlenecks is essential because they directly affect the cost per workflow: higher contention leads to longer execution times, increasing resource consumption and overall cost. Therefore, capacity planning must prioritize mitigating contention through sharding strategies and ensuring sufficient headroom for tool work, rather than merely scaling model compute resources.",
