      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Capacity Findings",
      "content": "The capacity evaluation data provides concrete performance metrics for various configurations. A kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while keeping utilization under 68 percent, indicating that the host can handle a moderate load without saturating resources (see [chunk-26136]). In the embedding evaluation, the system sustained 980 shards per second with utilization under 28 percent, suggesting ample headroom for additional tool workloads (see [chunk-26136]). Several contention points were resolved by sharding: pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas required 78-way sharding ([chunk-26128]); attention contention at 2,544 concurrent sampling needed 10-way sharding ([chunk-26111]); quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent median required 18-way sharding ([chunk-26110]). These sharding actions moved the performance ceilings upward, allowing higher concurrency without degradation. Additionally, the dataset team reported cache memory degradation beyond 4,133 backlog units and recommended raising the dataset memory budget to 62, while the attention team suggested increasing the attention latency budget to 39 once saturation crossed 2,182 units ([chunk-26108], [chunk-26132]). These specific numbers guide the sizing of memory and latency budgets for future host provisioning.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26111]", "[chunk-26110]", "[chunk-26108]", "[chunk-26132]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Analysis of Sharding Strategies",
      "content": "Across the field notes, sharding emerges as the primary mitigation technique for contention. Different pools required varying degrees of sharding: pipeline needed 78 ways, attention 10 ways, quantization 18 ways, and softmax 75 ways ([chunk-26128], [chunk-26111], [chunk-26110], [chunk-26119]). The effectiveness of sharding can be measured by the shift in performance ceilings. For example, after sharding the pipeline pool, the latency ceiling moved from a bound at 45 profile to a new ceiling of 45 profile, effectively eliminating the regression ([chunk-26128]). Similarly, sharding the attention pool moved the ceiling from 22 to a higher bound, allowing 2,544 concurrent sampling without latency spikes ([chunk-26111]). The comparative data suggests that larger sharding factors (e.g., 78-way pipeline) are required for pools with higher concurrency demands, while smaller factors (e.g., 10-way attention) suffice for less contended resources. This informs a tiered sharding approach: allocate more shards to high‑traffic pools and fewer to lower‑traffic ones, optimizing both memory overhead and performance.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26111]", "[chunk-26110]", "[chunk-26119]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications for Capacity Planning",
      "content": "The findings imply that capacity planning for agent hosts must be driven by the maximum concurrent tool operations rather than model inference load. With the kernel configuration handling 1,149 backlog per second at <68% utilization and the embedding configuration handling 980 shards per second at <28% utilization, a safe planning target is to provision for at least 1,200 concurrent tool operations. This buffer accommodates the observed contention thresholds (e.g., 3,312 concurrent pipeline replicas) and the recommended memory budgets (dataset memory 62, attention latency 39). Moreover, the need for extensive sharding across multiple pools suggests that host hardware should support a high number of logical partitions, such as CPUs with many cores and ample NUMA nodes, to enable efficient pool isolation. Failure to provision sufficient sharding capacity will result in regressions similar to those documented, leading to increased latency, higher cost per workflow, and potential SLA violations.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26111]", "[chunk-26110]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
