      "title": "Technical Details of Tool Workload",
      "content": "Tool work varies widely in resource intensity. CPU-bound tools such as data parsing, transformation, or custom business logic typically consume 0.5–2 CPU cores per concurrent execution, depending on algorithmic complexity. I/O-bound tools—like external API calls or database queries—use less CPU but require sufficient network sockets and memory buffers to handle latency spikes. Empirical measurements from the field indicate that a single orchestration host can sustain approximately 250 concurrent tool executions before CPU utilization reaches 80%, at which point response times begin to degrade. Memory usage scales linearly with the number of active agents, averaging 150 MB per agent for state tracking and result caching. Network bandwidth consumption is proportional to API payload size; a typical workflow involving three API calls of 200 KB each consumes roughly 600 KB per second per concurrent agent. These metrics provide a concrete baseline for sizing hosts: a 32‑core CPU with 128 GB RAM can comfortably support up to 200 agents under mixed workloads, maintaining a safety margin for burst traffic.",
