      "title": "Comparative Analysis of Host Configurations",
      "content": "When comparing standard cloud instance families, the cost‑to‑performance ratio favors compute‑optimized instances for tool‑heavy workloads. For example, a c5.4xlarge (16 vCPU, 32 GB RAM) can handle roughly 120 concurrent agents, whereas a memory‑optimized r5.4xlarge (16 vCPU, 128 GB RAM) supports only a modest increase to 140 agents due to CPU bottlenecks. Conversely, a balanced instance like m5.4xlarge (16 vCPU, 64 GB RAM) offers a middle ground, supporting 130 agents with acceptable latency. Scaling horizontally by adding identical hosts yields linear throughput gains, provided load balancers distribute tool calls evenly. However, vertical scaling beyond 32 cores shows diminishing returns because tool work often stalls on I/O, not raw CPU cycles. Therefore, a hybrid approach—multiple compute‑optimized nodes with shared state storage—delivers the best cost efficiency while preserving resilience.",
