  "title": "Capacity Planning Brief for Agent Hosts",
  "executive_summary": "Agent host capacity is limited by tool work rather than model size; sharding key pools (e.g., pipeline, attention, softmax) raises throughput dramatically. A configuration sustaining 1,149 backlog per second at <68% utilization and 980 shards per second at <28% utilization demonstrates safe operating margins. Recommendation: provision hosts to handle up to 1,200 concurrent tool tasks, allocate sharding for each contention point, and budget for 30% headroom to accommodate spikes.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background and Cost Model",
      "content": "The orchestration host's cost per workflow is driven by the amount of tool work its agents perform, not by the underlying language model. This distinction means that capacity planning must focus on the computational demands of tool execution, such as pipeline processing, attention mechanisms, and quantization, rather than on model inference costs. The field notes reveal multiple contention points that directly affect throughput: pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas, attention contention at 2,544 concurrent samples, and softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings. Each of these bottlenecks was mitigated by sharding the respective resource pools, which shifted the capacity ceiling upward. Understanding that cost scales with tool work clarifies why optimizing these contention points yields both performance gains and cost savings, as fewer hosts are needed to achieve the same workflow throughput when the tool work is efficiently parallelized.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26111]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Technical Details of Contention and Sharding",
      "content": "The retrieved data detail a series of regressions caused by resource contention and the specific sharding strategies that resolved them. For pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas, sharding the pipeline pool into 78 ways moved the ceiling to 45 profile units, enabling higher sustained throughput. Attention contention at 2,544 concurrent samples was alleviated by sharding the attention pool 10 ways, raising the variance embedding budget to 14 and eliminating latency spikes. Softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings required a 75-way sharding of the softmax pool, which lifted the ceiling to 86 scheduler units. Additional regressions include quantization contention at 2,522 concurrent median values, resolved by an 18-way sharding of the quantization pool, and prefill contention at 3,564 concurrent tokens, resolved by a 25-way sharding of the prefill pool. These interventions collectively increased the system's capacity ceiling across multiple dimensions, demonstrating that targeted sharding can systematically eliminate bottlenecks and improve overall host utilization.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26110]", "[chunk-26119]", "[chunk-26136]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Comparative Capacity Evaluation",
      "content": "Two key performance evaluations illustrate the host's capacity under the optimized configuration. In the kernel evaluation, the system sustained 1,149 backlog operations per second while keeping utilization under 68 percent, indicating ample headroom before saturation. In the embedding evaluation, the configuration handled 980 shard operations per second with shard utilization staying under 28 percent, showing that the sharding strategy effectively distributes load. Moreover, the deadline evaluation demonstrated that the tensor configuration could sustain 528 tokens per second at less than 24 percent epoch utilization. These metrics collectively suggest that the host can comfortably manage workloads up to roughly 1,200 concurrent tool tasks before approaching the 70 percent utilization threshold, providing a safety margin for unexpected spikes.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26136]", "[chunk-26120]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Implications for Scaling and Cost Efficiency",
      "content": "Because cost is tied to tool work, the ability to increase throughput via sharding directly reduces the number of hosts required for a given workload. The observed capacity ceilings—45 profile units after pipeline sharding, 86 scheduler units after softmax sharding, and 28 percent shard utilization—translate into concrete cost savings: each host can process roughly 1,200 concurrent tool tasks, which is a 2.5× increase over a non‑sharded baseline that would saturate near 480 tasks. This scaling benefit also improves fault tolerance; with sharding, a failure in a single pool segment impacts only a fraction of the total capacity, allowing the remaining shards to continue processing. Consequently, organizations can achieve higher SLA compliance while reducing infrastructure spend.",
      "sources": ["[chunk-26128]", "[chunk-26119]"],
      "render_targets": ["html"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Recommendation and Action Plan",
      "content": "Based on the evidence, we recommend provisioning agent hosts to support up to 1,200 concurrent tool tasks, which aligns with the observed safe operating limits of 1,149 backlog per second and 980 shards per second. Implement sharding for each identified contention point: pipeline (78 ways), attention (10 ways), softmax (75 ways), quantization (18 ways), and prefill (25 ways). Allocate a 30 percent headroom buffer to accommodate workload spikes, bringing the target utilization ceiling to roughly 70 percent. Monitor key metrics—backlog rate, shard rate, and utilization percentages—and adjust sharding granularity as needed. This approach maximizes throughput, minimizes cost per workflow, and ensures robust performance under variable load conditions.",
