  "executive_summary": "Agent hosts must be sized based on the tool work their agents perform, not the underlying model cost. Recent capacity evaluations show that a kernel configuration can sustain 1,149 backlog per second at under 68% utilization, while an embedding configuration sustains 980 shards per second at under 28% utilization. Sharding critical resource pools (e.g., pipeline, attention, softmax) consistently resolves contention and raises ceilings, enabling higher throughput without exceeding 70% utilization. Recommendation: provision hosts to handle the peak tool‑work load (≈1,200 backlog/sec) with a 30% headroom, and implement automatic sharding of identified contention points to maintain utilization below 70%.",
  "sections": [
    {
      "title": "Background and Cost Model",
      "content": "The orchestration host's cost per workflow is determined by the tool work its agents execute, not by the AI model itself. This distinction means that capacity planning should focus on the computational demands of the agents' tool invocations—such as data preprocessing, embedding generation, and result aggregation—rather than the inference cost of the language model. In practice, this shifts the planning horizon from model licensing fees to hardware provisioning for high‑throughput tool pipelines. The field notes emphasize that the cost structure is directly tied to the volume and complexity of tool work, making it essential to quantify the maximum concurrent tool operations the host can sustain while keeping utilization within safe margins. Understanding this cost model is the first step toward designing a host architecture that can reliably meet service level objectives without incurring unnecessary expense.",
