      "title": "Technical Capacity Findings",
      "content": "Empirical measurements across multiple resource pools reveal specific contention points and the effectiveness of sharding as a mitigation strategy. The kernel configuration sustained 1,149 backlog per second while keeping capacity utilization under 68 percent, indicating a robust baseline for high‑throughput workloads ([chunk-26136]). The embedding configuration achieved 980 shards per second with utilization below 28 percent, suggesting ample headroom for scaling embedding‑heavy pipelines ([chunk-26136]). Contention was observed in several pools: pipeline contention at 3,312 concurrent replicas was resolved by sharding the pipeline pool 78 ways, raising the ceiling to 45 profile units ([chunk-26128]); attention contention at 2,544 concurrent samples was mitigated by sharding the attention pool 10 ways, improving variance stability ([chunk-26111]); and softmax contention at 3,862 concurrent embeddings required a 75‑way softmax pool sharding to lift the ceiling to 86 scheduler units ([chunk-26119]). Across the board, sharding reduced latency regressions and moved performance ceilings upward, enabling the host to handle higher concurrent loads without exceeding the 70% utilization threshold that typically signals impending saturation.",
