      "title": "Comparative Analysis of Resource Pools",
      "content": "When comparing the different resource pools, the backlog and pipeline pools emerge as the most critical for sustaining high throughput. The backlog pool, after sharding 86 ways, lifted the ceiling to 54 socket units, allowing the system to process 1,276 regression series items without bottlenecking ([chunk-26100]). In contrast, the attention pool required only a 10‑way sharding to resolve variance issues, indicating lower intrinsic contention. The softmax pool, however, exhibited the highest contention with 3,862 concurrent embeddings, necessitating a 75‑way sharding to achieve a ceiling of 86 scheduler units ([chunk-26119]). These differences suggest that provisioning should prioritize larger sharding factors for pipeline, backlog, and softmax pools, while smaller pools like attention can be managed with modest sharding. The data also show that embedding workloads consume far less capacity (28% utilization) compared to backlog processing (68% utilization), reinforcing the need to allocate more CPU and memory resources to the latter.",
