    {
      "title": "Implications for Host Sizing and Scaling",
      "content": "Given the observed ceilings and utilization rates, a safe sizing rule is to provision hosts that can sustain at least 1,200 backlog operations per second, providing a 30% buffer above the measured 1,149 backlog/sec capacity. This buffer accounts for traffic spikes and the occasional regression in latency that may arise from unanticipated tool work patterns. Additionally, hosts should be equipped with automatic sharding mechanisms for the pipeline, softmax, and backlog pools, as these have demonstrated the greatest impact on performance ceilings. By maintaining utilization below 70% across all pools, the system can avoid the latency regressions documented at higher contention levels (e.g., 3,312 concurrent replicas in the pipeline pool). Scaling out should follow a horizontal model: add identical hosts once the primary host reaches 70% utilization, ensuring that each new node inherits the same sharding configuration to preserve performance consistency.",
